From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:13:59 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 23/42] hw/usb: Add the i.MX21 USB host controller

Replace the register-only model of the i.MX21 USB OTG block with a
host controller that does transfers, on a QEMU USB bus with three
full-speed root hub ports, so -device usb-storage, usb-kbd, usb-mouse,
usb-tablet, usb-audio, usb-hub and the like attach to it. The device
keeps its type name, address and interrupt (IRQ 55), and moves to
hw/usb.

The stock 2.6.16 "imx21-hc" driver works like this. It gives each
endpoint one of 32 endpoint transfer descriptors (ETDs) at offset 0x200:
word 0 holds address, endpoint, direction, speed, format and max
packet, word 1 the X/Y buffers in the 4 KiB data memory (DMEM) at
offset 0x1000, word 2 the interval, toggle and completion code, word 3
the byte count. For control, bulk and interrupt transfers it points the
ETD's DMA start address (0x900 + 4n) at a kernel buffer and enables the
ETD's DMA. Isochronous ETDs, two per endpoint, carry a start frame and
one packet; the stock snd-usb-audio buffers go through DMEM, others
through the DMA. Then it sets the ETD's bit in ETDENSET. When it is
done the controller sets the bit in ETDDONESTAT, which the driver only
scans on a SOF interrupt. It reads back the completion code, the
residual count and the toggle carry. Control transfers are three ETD
runs (setup, data, status). The status, fill and DMA enable registers
are "toggling": a 1 written flips the bit.

The model runs a control, bulk or interrupt ETD as one USB packet for
the whole byte count, with the data moved by the DMA (or from DMEM).
It writes back the completion code, residual count and toggle. A NAKed
ETD is retried on later frames: every frame for bulk, every polling
interval for interrupt, and at once when the device signals new data.
Asynchronous packets (usb-storage) complete from the port's complete
callback. An isochronous ETD runs in its start frame, oldest first.
Frames are 1 ms of virtual time and FRMNUB counts them. A done ETD
raises the done and SOF interrupts, and SOF repeats each frame while a
done status is still pending. Port status registers follow OHCI:
write-1-to-act, write-1-to-clear change bits, and resets that finish
at once. The function side, HNP and the OTG block's I2C master are not
modelled. Chumby's board code doesn't use the transceiver I2C master;
its setup is under "#if 0".

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5Fc77Z6fVVe4rWJxPT
